+# Source Audit
+
+`source-audit` is HSE's deterministic, evidence-first source-code contradiction scanner.
+It is **development/CI tooling**, not part of the OSINT runtime, and it does not call an
+LLM or a remote analysis service.
+
+## Epistemic contract
+
+A rule match is a **candidate hypothesis**, not a confirmed defect or vulnerability.
+Every finding carries the exact source span that triggered it, a rationale, concrete
+verification steps, and explicit falsification conditions. The tool deliberately makes
+it harder to convert a plausible pattern into a false security claim.
+
+The initial rule set targets contradiction classes that conventional linting often
+misses and that have produced real defects in heavily analysed systems:
+
+- `intent.remove_vs_xor` — comments/contracts say remove/disable/clear while code uses
+  XOR toggling (`^=`), which cannot guarantee a cleared post-state.
+- `contract.case_insensitive_exact_compare` — a nearby case-insensitive contract is
+  followed by an exact string comparison without visible normalization.
+- `units.mixed_time_compare` — a comparison directly mixes identifiers labelled with
+  different time units, such as nanoseconds and milliseconds.
+- `contract.optional_pointer_deref` — C/C++ documentation says a backticked pointer
+  parameter is optional/null while a later dereference has no visible preceding guard.
+
+All rules are deterministic and bounded. Files larger than 2 MiB are skipped by default;
+`.git`, `target`, `vendor`, `node_modules`, `dist`, `build`, and `.worktrees` are excluded.
+Rust and C-family source files are supported in the first version.
+
+## Usage
+
+```bash
+cargo run --bin source-audit -- .
+cargo run --bin source-audit -- . --json --out source-audit.json
+cargo run --bin source-audit -- src/core --context-lines 8
+```
+
+For CI experiments, `--fail-on-findings` returns exit code 1 when candidates exist. It is
+opt-in because candidate presence alone is not enough evidence to reject a change.
+
+When run inside a Git working tree, the report records `git rev-parse HEAD` and whether the
+worktree is dirty. A clean SHA is replayable; a dirty worktree is explicitly labelled as
+not replayable by SHA alone.
+
+## Verification workflow
+
+For every candidate:
+
+1. Trace the actual data/control flow around the cited span.
+2. Attempt the finding's listed falsification conditions first.
+3. Reproduce the disputed behavior using the real parser/state machine/build path.
+4. Retain the finding only if the contradiction survives falsification.
+5. Report confirmed defects separately from heuristic candidates.
+
+The design is intentionally compatible with later external-agent orchestration: JSON
+output is a deterministic evidence pack an authorized analysis agent can consume, while
+the core HSE runtime remains AI-independent.
